ation certain regional law schools  punch way above their weight for the  region that they're located in if you  know you want to end up in a particular  location you might go to a law school in  that area the alumni network might be  well-connected in that area you might be  able to get  more networking opportunities in that  city or that state so in certain  circumstances a regional law school can  have advantages over a higher ranked Law  School so for example if you knew you  wanted to practice in Los Angeles  California you might consider going to  Loyola or Pepperdine in the LA area and  while they're not the top-ranked law  schools in the country they punch above  their weight in the Los Angeles area by  the same token however going to a much  higher ranked law school might open up  even more opportunities in a wider  geographical location and frankly if you  go to some of the top law schools then  it shouldn't be too difficult to get a  job in any particular location but  that's always something to consider  especially if different law schools  offer different financial packages and  financial aid as well sometimes going to  a regional school can save you a lot of  money but you have to weigh the pros and  cons versus going to a better ranked  school that might open up more doors  outside of just a particular  geographical location and sometimes  people decide that they don't want to  stay in a particular city or state and  they want to move to somewhere else  going to a higher ranked school can  sometimes open it up those kind of  opportunities which brings me to the  next factor that you should probably  consider when deciding between law  schools and that's how much are you  willing to pay unfortunately there's not  much difference in terms of the public  or RAK rates that you see from different  law schools and the amount that you pay  doesn't really correlate to the rank of  that school in fact it's often inversely  correlated where the better schools  