 whether  you go to the number one law school in  the country or the number one hundredth  law school in the country it's shocking  but they all teach basically the same  thing but a handful of schools really  excel in a few specialty areas like tax  or entertainment law or law and  economics so if you know that you want  to go into one of these specialty fields  that generally the kind of fields that  require an LLM or a masters of the law  then you might consider going to one of  those schools that really excels in that  area but outside of those really  specialty areas I would not assume that  any law school is going to teach you  anything different from another law  school and unless you're going into one  of those LLM type areas odds are you're  gonna get the exact same education as  anywhere else there aren't that many  areas in which this specialty applies  tax is the one that really comes out so  if you know that you want to go into one  of these specialty fields that require  is an advanced law degree then you might  consider going to a law school that has  a very high ranked specialty program in  that area  the next big consideration when thinking  about law schools is whether you want to  go into big  most students think that they want to go  into big law when they start law school  whether it's actually the best thing for  them or not and while it's certainly not  the route for everyone it is the route  that I took I've been a big firm lawyer  for over ten years I've recruited I have  mentored summer associates and here's  what I think are the most important  takeaways if you want to go into big law  when you're deciding between law schools  if you want to go the big firm route you  generally need to go to one of the best  schools in the country the most  prestigious schools at the top of the  250 law schools in the country there are  three at the very top that are always  changing in position for number one and  that's usually Yale Harvard in Stanford  which one of those three schools is  ranked number one in a particular year  largely depends on who you ask but  generally those three schools are  considered the top three schools in the  country if you're able to get into the  roughly top half of your class at one of  the top law schools in the country you  stand a very good chance of being  competitive with the top firms in the  country and getting a big firm job now  apart from the top three law schools  there are what people generally refer to  as the top tier of law schools we often  call the tier one law schools or t14 law  schools those are also almost  universally considered the top law  schools in the country roughly the top  10% you'll hear people talk about the  top 14 law schools in the country  sometimes as its top 15 and sometimes  it's the top 20 really there's not a  whole lot of difference between those  top tier schools those top 10% of law  schools or schools like NYU Berkeley  UCLA Georgetown Michigan Duke Columbia  I'm also leaving out a whole bunch of  those top tier schools but they're  generally considered the top schools in  the country even though you're in one of  the top law schools in the country you  still need to do reasonably well in your  class in order to get a big firm job but  if you're safely in the top third of  your class at a tier one school you  stand a very good chance of getting a  big firm job or a clerkship after that I  would say that there is sort of a second  tier of law schools the law schools  ranked generally between 20 and 100 if  you go to one of these schools  and you want to go the big firm route  generally you have to be in the top five  to ten percent of your class you have to  do very well at these tier two schools  in order to get the most competitive  jobs because you're competing with  thousands of people that went to the top  tier schools and you have to demonstrate  that you are above the rest of your  class so it can be very difficult if you  want to go to a law school that is  outside the top 100 and you still want  to go the the prestigious big firm route  well it's still possible but you're  really going to have to differentiate  yourself from the rest of your class you  really have to excel and be roughly the  top one or two people maybe the top five  in your class in order to get the kind  of ranking that would allow you to get  the big firm jobs these are the kind of  rankings and grades that are also going  to let you transfer to a different  school if you want to go that route but  keep in mind that big law is not all  there is there's all kinds of jobs in  law and you shouldn't assume that big  law is necessarily the right one for you  you've got to work thousands of hours  and it can really burn you out so don't  just assume that big law is the one and  only route in law school there's lots of  different careers in legal field and you  may not want to go into big law in  particular additionally when you're  thinking about different careers in law  check out the data that the ABA puts out  on different law schools the ABA  publishes information on each school it  will tell you how successful their  graduates are what states that those  graduates practice in and it will also  tell you the average income that  graduates from that particular law  school make so don't just rely on  generalities but really dig in and see  if graduates from the law school that  you're considering are going into the  kind of jobs that you really want to go  into so that being said well everyone's  situation is different and there's never  a general best law school for everyone I  do want to give you some general tips  all things being equal I would say go to  a higher ranked law school the higher  ranked law schools especially when you  get into the top tier of law schools  really open up many different options  for you and maybe a year from now when  you're a 2l or three years from now what  you want out of your legal career might  be completely different than one  you want when you start out so going to  a higher ranked law firm often if  there's a big difference in the ranking  can really open up some different  options if not now then perhaps later  down the line so all things being equal  you might consider a higher ranked law  school also again all things being equal  you might consider going to a school  that offers more financial aid if you're  deciding between schools within the top  tier or within the top three and the top  tier and one school gives you a full  ride and another school gives you zero  financial aid I would probably choose  the one that gives you more financial  aid despite the fact that there might be  a 10 rank difference between school  number one in school number ten there's  probably not going to be that much of a  difference in terms of your employment  prospects and certainly very little in  terms of what's actually taught at the  law school so you could do yourself a  huge favor by taking money for the  slightly lower ranked school if that  comes with more financial aid and you'd  probably be very happy to graduate  debt-free from a top-tier law school if  the alternative was to go to another  top-ranked Floss school with a ton of  debt often that can be the difference  between zero debt and $250,000 worth of  debt and when you graduate you know if  you don't go into big law and you go  into a legal career that pays you know  seventy or eighty thousand dollars which  is the median salary for an attorney  having or not having two hundred and  fifty thousand dollars worth of loans to  repay over a ten year time period can  make all the difference in your quality  of life again all things being equal go  to a law school that is closer to the  geographical location that you know you  want to practice if you know that you  want to practice in Boston then by all  means go to a law school in Boston it's  very helpful to be able to interview  with a lot of firms in the area in which  you're going to law school otherwise the  alternative might be having to fly  across the country in order to do the  same number of interviews or or to  interview with the same type of firm  with law schools don't tell you is that  at the beginning of your second year  you're going to be doing a ton of  interviews with firms and  governmental agencies to try and line up  the job that you have when you graduate  from law school and it is so much easier  to be able to take on-campus interviews  with the type of firms that you want to  practice in the future because local  firms are more likely to hold on campus  interviews at a local law school it's  just the economics of the situation so  going to a law school in the city or the  state where you want to practice can  make all the difference in your second  year when you're taking interviews for  the firms that you want to work for in  the future so this is just the tip of  the iceberg in terms of what you should  do before law school if you want more  strategies and tactics for what to do  before starting law school download our  free ultimate pre-law checklist it's our  gift to you the link to that is down  below and it will give you a head start  when you get to law school also please  click the like and subscribe buttons the  more people that subscribe to this  channel the more videos we can make on  how to crush law school so did I miss  any considerations that you have about  choosing your law school are you  choosing between law school right now  have you already decided let me know in  the comments below I would love to hear  from you and until next time at legal  eagle think like a lawyer and ace your  exams ah the age-old question of how to  choose the right law school but today  we're gonna go over the main concerns  that you should consider when deciding  between one so before you think about  what you want so before you think about  so before you think about

